# 6. Kubernetes Storage

Containers are temporary.

Example:

```text
Container

↓

Application Data

↓

Container Deleted

↓

Data Lost
```

For permanent data, Kubernetes uses storage objects.

---

# 7. Volume

A Volume provides storage to containers.

Example:

```text
Pod

|

Container

|

Volume

|

Storage
```

---

Types:

* emptyDir
* hostPath
* Persistent Volume
* Cloud storage

---

# 8. emptyDir Volume

Temporary storage.

Created when Pod starts.

Deleted when Pod is removed.

Example:

```yaml
volumes:

- name: cache

  emptyDir: {}
```

Use cases:

* Temporary files
* Cache

---

# 9. Persistent Volume (PV)

A Persistent Volume is storage created by an administrator or provisioner.

Examples:

AWS:

* EBS
* EFS

Architecture:

```text
Pod

↓

PVC

↓

PV

↓

AWS Storage
```

---

# 10. Persistent Volume Claim (PVC)

A PVC is a request for storage.

Application says:

"I need 20GB storage"

PVC finds matching PV.

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1

kind: PersistentVolumeClaim

metadata:

 name: database-storage


spec:

 accessModes:

 - ReadWriteOnce


 resources:

  requests:

   storage: 20Gi
```

---

# 11. StorageClass

StorageClass provides dynamic storage creation.

Without StorageClass:

```text
Admin

↓

Create PV

↓

Application uses PV
```

With StorageClass:

```text
Application

↓

PVC

↓

StorageClass

↓

Automatically creates PV
```

---

# 12. AWS Storage in EKS

Common options:

## Amazon EBS

Block storage.

Good for:

* Databases
* Single-node storage

Example:

```text
MySQL Pod

↓

EBS Volume
```

---

## Amazon EFS

Shared file system.

Good for:

* Multiple Pods
* Shared files

Example:

```text
Pod 1
  |
  |
EFS
  |
  |
Pod 2
```

---

# 13. StatefulSet

Deployments are for stateless applications.

Example:

* Web servers
* APIs

Databases need stable identity.

StatefulSet provides:

* Stable Pod names
* Stable storage
* Ordered deployment

---

Example:

```text
mysql-0

mysql-1

mysql-2
```

Each Pod keeps identity.

---

# 14. StatefulSet Example

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1

kind: StatefulSet

metadata:

 name: mysql


spec:

 serviceName: mysql

 replicas: 3


 template:

  spec:

   containers:

   - name: mysql

     image: mysql
```

---

# 15. DaemonSet

DaemonSet runs one Pod on every node.

Example:

```text
Node 1

↓

Logging Agent


Node 2

↓

Logging Agent
```

Used for:

* Monitoring agents
* Log collectors
* Security agents

---

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1

kind: DaemonSet

metadata:

 name: fluent-bit
```

---

# 16. Jobs

Jobs run tasks that complete.

Examples:

* Database migration
* Backup
* Batch processing

Flow:

```text
Job

↓

Pod Created

↓

Task Completed

↓

Pod Stops
```

---

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: batch/v1

kind: Job

metadata:

 name: backup-job
```

---

# 17. CronJob

CronJob runs Jobs on a schedule.

Example:

Daily backup:

```text
Every day

2 AM

↓

Backup Job
```

---

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: batch/v1

kind: CronJob

metadata:

 name: daily-backup


spec:

 schedule:

  "0 2 * * *"
```

---

# 18. Kubernetes Probes

Kubernetes needs to know application health.

Three types:

---

# Liveness Probe

Checks:

"Is the application alive?"

If failed:

```text
Restart Container
```

Example:

```yaml
livenessProbe:

 httpGet:

  path: /

  port: 80
```

---

# Readiness Probe

Checks:

"Can this application receive traffic?"

If failed:

```text
Remove from Service
```

---

# Startup Probe

Used for slow applications.

Example:

* Large Java applications
* Machine learning models

---

# 19. Resource Requests and Limits

Containers need CPU and memory.

Example:

```yaml
resources:

 requests:

  memory: "256Mi"

  cpu: "500m"


 limits:

  memory: "512Mi"

  cpu: "1"
```

---

## Request

Minimum resources required.

---

## Limit

Maximum resources allowed.

---

# 20. Kubernetes Scheduling

Scheduler decides where Pods run.

Factors:

* CPU
* Memory
* Labels
* Rules

---

# 21. Node Selector

Run Pod on specific node.

Example:

Node:

```text
environment=gpu
```

Pod:

```yaml
nodeSelector:

 environment: gpu
```

---

# 22. Taints and Tolerations

Used to control Pod placement.

Example:

GPU node:

```text
No normal Pods allowed
```

Add taint:

```bash
kubectl taint nodes gpu-node gpu=true:NoSchedule
```

Pod needs matching toleration.

---

# 23. Affinity

Affinity provides advanced scheduling rules.

Example:

"Run application close to database."

Architecture:

```text
Application Pod

↓

Same Node/AZ

↓

Database Pod
```

---

# 24. Ingress

Ingress manages external HTTP traffic.

Without Ingress:

```text
Application 1

↓

Load Balancer


Application 2

↓

Load Balancer
```

Many load balancers.

---

With Ingress:

```text
Internet

↓

Ingress Controller

↓

Application Services
```

---

Example:

```text
example.com/api

↓

Backend Service


example.com/web

↓

Frontend Service
```

---

# 25. AWS Ingress in EKS

EKS commonly uses:

AWS Load Balancer Controller

Flow:

```text
User

↓

Application Load Balancer

↓

Ingress

↓

Service

↓

Pods
```

---

# 26. Network Policies

Network Policies control Pod communication.

Default:

```text
All Pods can communicate
```

With Network Policy:

```text
Frontend

✔

Backend


Frontend

✘

Database
```

---

Example:

Allow only frontend:

```yaml
kind: NetworkPolicy

spec:

 podSelector:

  matchLabels:

   app: database
```

---

# 27. Kubernetes Security Practices

Recommended:

* Use RBAC
* Avoid running as root
* Use Secrets
* Scan container images
* Use Network Policies
* Limit resources
* Enable audit logging

